A||||17||pcdude||Should I get a fiero????????||||5||
Z||000000||pcdude||12-16-2001||05:05 PM||||I'm 16 and will get a car soon hopefully. So how good are Fiero's? Do they need a lot of maintenance and if so how much. I live in Washington so it will be raining a lot, how good is it in the rain? Also I want a GT, what year should I get, is the 88 the one with the good suspension designed by Lotus? Thanks for all the help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroMontreal||12-16-2001||05:12 PM||||Watch out for these crazy death traps! They burn up when it's hot outside and they Melt under the rain.. Just ask AusFiero .. He had the heater on a little to high in this garage and the car melted away!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>If you do buy one make sure to check the wheels because they tend to fly of when you go over 60mph ... <P>make sure you get an 88 .. all the other years did not use any side impact beams.. therefor if you ever got hit on the side by an other car the only thing that was their to stop you was the plastic door panel and the window.. GM engineers thought that the plastic/rubber panels would be strong enough to resist any type of impact but they were wrong!!<P>oh and don't buy a red fiero!! Fire In Every Red One!!<P>Each Fiero has at least one Gremlin.. These little creatures are known to cause electrical problems. Watch out for them.. they sleep during the day but come out at night to cause trouble in the engine bay.. Legend has it that the gremlins were placed in Fiero's by an ex worker from the Pinto plant.. no one knows why he did it.. all that we know is that he was an evil man giving us problems we shouldn't have.<P><BR>good luck!<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> -Alex <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontrealholidays.gif">   <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> <BR>and Regional Director of CFOG-I for Eastern Canada.<P>[This message has been edited by FieroMontreal (edited 12-16-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by FieroMontreal (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Spektrum-87GT||12-16-2001||05:12 PM||||the 88s have a different suspension than the 84-87s... it was NOT designed by lotus<P>gts are great! but i'm biased... obviously... get an 86-87 if you want a cheaper fastback... the 88 is the best though, just a lot more expensive and harder to find certain parts(suspension) for.<P>good luck with your search and welcome to the forums<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/spektrum_87gt/" TARGET=_blank>87 Fiero GT</A><BR>1991 Jeep Cherokee<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.com/bspracklin1/sig2.jpg">   <BR>AIM:<FONT COLOR="red">S</FONT><FONT COLOR="purple">p</FONT><FONT COLOR="blue">e</FONT><FONT COLOR="cyan">k</FONT><FONT COLOR="green">t</FONT><FONT COLOR="yellow">r</FONT><FONT COLOR="orange">u</FONT><FONT COLOR="red">m</FONT><FONT COLOR="purple">1</FONT><FONT COLOR="blue">1</FONT><FONT COLOR="cyan">7</FONT><BR>And no! i'm not gay dangit!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jake_Dragon||12-16-2001||05:15 PM||||Lotus?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/sad2.gif">  Welcome to the board.<BR>88 was the best year but mostly because they had 4 more years to work on them.<BR>What do you know about cars? Like any older car it will need work sooner or later and it would be better if you know how to work on it.<BR>Should make a great Father and Son project.<BR>I have two and drive them daily.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||12-16-2001||05:17 PM||||no, bad idea, big mistake, money down th...<P>wait...washington. that means what ever car you find i probaly can't get ahold of anyway.<P>ok. go ahead and get one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||avengador1||12-16-2001||05:20 PM||||You do know that he was kidding? Right? You have to remember that some of these cars are over 14 years old so it goes without saying, they will need some work. Try to get one that has low mileage and good service records. Make sure all the body panels align and don't have any uneven gaps. Pull the rug out of the trunk and check for rust. Look underneath and check for dents and scrape marks. You don't want a car that has been in an accident or that has excessive rust, these will need a whole lot of work to fix. For economy get a 4 cylinder model over the 6. To avoid clutch problems get an automatic transmission model. You can always bolt in another engine latter. Hope this helps.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||REDHOTT88||12-16-2001||05:37 PM||||Welcome to the Fiero Forum!!<P>You want to know if you should buy a FIERO?<BR>Did you notice this was the "Fiero" Forum?<P>No bias, but the 88's are best!!! (and they DID have input from Lotus!!)<P>As with any "classic" car, you have to decide, do I want to pay to keep this car in good condition? Or do I want a nice dependable new car?<P>A Fiero is not a Civic, Escort or Vw Jetta.<BR>It's a hobby. If you're fairly good at working on cars, and like it, a Fiero will bring you many rewards.<P>Other than that buy a new "Ricer" and just keep adding plastic accessories and decals to it from Pep Boys!!!<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/bangin.gif">   <P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">         <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||12-16-2001||06:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>Welcome to the Fiero Forum!!<P>You want to know if you should buy a FIERO?<BR>Did you notice this was the "Fiero" Forum?<P>No bias, but the 88's are best!!! (and they DID have input from Lotus!!)<P>As with any "classic" car, you have to decide, do I want to pay to keep this car in good condition? Or do I want a nice dependable new car?<P>A Fiero is not a Civic, Escort or Vw Jetta.<BR>It's a hobby. If you're fairly good at working on cars, and like it, a Fiero will bring you many rewards.<P>Other than that buy a new "Ricer" and just keep adding plastic accessories and decals to it from Pep Boys!!!<P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/bangin.gif">    <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Just to clarify... The ONLY input GM had was a lotus car that they LOOKED at when designing the system. Uses a SIMILAR, but completely independantly researched design... How do these rumors get started?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I love my car. It's good to me. Small things break all the time, as to be expected with a 14-year old car. HOWEVER, the parts are STOOPID-Cheap. <P>If you are on ANY form of budget, I would steer away from the 88s. The single model-year run has resulted in parts that are almost impossible to acquire, whereas the 84-87 are DEAD easy. Also, for the money that you'd put into restoring an 88 suspension, you can realistically mod out the 84-87 to be comparable...<P>Other than the suspension differences, the internals haven't changed MUCH over the years. If not abused, and obsessively maintained, the Fiero will be MORE than nice to you. It's an eye-turner. More so if in great condition!<P>Have fun, and ignore the clowns on this forum giving you a hard time... Civility seems somewhat LACKING today  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>||||reg||12
Z||000008||JohnF||12-16-2001||06:34 PM||||An 84 - 87 Fiero probably cost about the same as a comparable Pinto or Vega.  Also cost about the same to maintain in dependable condition.  <P>So, which one would you rather have?<P>Remember, Pintos need love, too!||||reg||10
Z||000009||gargoyle||12-16-2001||06:42 PM||||Not a bad car but unless you buy that 88 on Ebay with only 1500 miles on it you better be prepared to do some work on it. I bought my 87 GT in July and so far I have had to do something every month but I think I have most of it covered now. I have replaced brakes, exhaust+ cat, water pump, alternator, headlight motors with relay switch, and I'm doing tires now(for winter). You just have to remember this is a 14-18 year old car. It is a lot of fun though. I don't regret buying it at all.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||johnt671||12-16-2001||07:26 PM||||Pinto?  Didn't they catch on fire a lot?  Wow, I always wanted to say that about another make of car!!!!  Got thrown out of a Ford dealer cause he was trying to sell a gf a pinto.  Just asked how many people were killed in them.  Salesman got insulted.  Buy a Fiero but be ready to work on it. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||GTFiero1||12-16-2001||07:28 PM||||hey cool i wanna GT for my first car too.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_3.jpg">   <BR>you gotta keep on keepin on...<BR>IM AOL: GTFiero &  Rumpdetective (dont ask)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||12-16-2001||07:31 PM||||One note about the side impact beam thing.. <B>every</B> Fiero came with not one, but two side impact beams in each door.  One down along the bottom and one at about shoulder height.  Which is the reason the doors weigh about 80 pounds each...<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
